WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.380
We cannot help someone while they're living in a tent.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.820
We need a triage-like approach.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:15.180
They're not doing it at nearly the intensity or the urgency or the scope that we need it.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.740
This is Skid Row, a 53 square block area of downtown Los Angeles,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.340
where more than 2500 homeless individuals reside on the streets.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.500
California's homeless population has jumped by more than 12% in the last five years,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.720
and it's part of a national crisis.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.760
Skid Row is what I call it the worst
manmade disaster in the United States.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.420
There's human waste on the sidewalks.
There's all kinds of disease.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.920
Actually I lost my right leg on the streets of Skid Row from getting a staph infection,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.160
from coming in contact with human waste.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.160
Reverend Andy Bales is the CEO of Union Rescue Mission,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.980
which is the nation's largest private homeless shelter.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.880
He says that the city of Los Angeles,

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.360
which has the largest unsheltered
homeless population in America,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.740
has failed to deal with what's become a
public health and humanitarian crisis.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.180
More than a thousand homeless people died on the streets of Los Angeles County last year

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.920
according to government figures.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.620
Meanwhile, the city is spending more than a billion dollars on a long-term housing solution

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.560
that likely will take more than a decade to complete.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.740
There's a particular type of TB found only in the world on Skid Row.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.180
We've had typhus, medieval disease carried by fleas on rats.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.900
When you leave people on the streets,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.680
they are quickly devastated by what they experience.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.740
Homelessness destroys people physically, mentally, emotionally,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.020
educationally, vocationally, every way you can think of.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.960
And in LA there's nowhere to go, right?

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:50.760
We put a roof over at 25% of the people devastated by homelessness, we leave 75% on the streets.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:57.380
Los Angeles is now coming together to confront the greatest moral and humanitarian crisis of our time.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.640
Homelessness.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.680
In 2016 Los Angeles voters approved a
referendum to spend more than $1.2 billion

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.680
building new housing for the homeless.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.560
It's part of a plan championed by Los
Angeles mayor Eric Garcetti,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.460
who declined our interview request.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:17.560
And I will accept nothing less than a home for every person who needs a roof over their head.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:23.140
We are here to end homelessness.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.200
The city set a target of 10,000 new housing units within a decade

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.800
that we're supposed to take between three and six years to build.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.760
But three years in just 1% of those apartments will be ready for occupancy by the end of 2019.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.800
It's going to be too late. When they get to spend the money,

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.020
they're going to be triple the homeless there are out here now.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.020
A lot of these projects are taking forever to build.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.760
They need to find something now. Look at
them. They don't have no place to go.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.460
Building anything in California isn't easy.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:56.840
The state's legislative analyst's office found that increasing competition for limited housing

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.980
is the primary driver of housing cost growth in coastal California,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.220
which has some of the nation's highest housing prices and rents

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.540
because local interest groups often use tools like zoning and the state's environmental review law

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.040
to delay or kill new housing projects.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:18.380
The shortage drives up prices and some living on the margins are priced out and turn to the streets.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.680
And even after voters approved the more than a billion dollars

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.240
specifically to build housing for the increasing homeless population,

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.440
a recent report by LA's controller's office attributed the delays

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:36.300
and cost overruns largely to regulatory barriers, permitting challenges, and bureaucratic confusion.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.820
Meanwhile, the existing shelters are running out of space,

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.940
threatening to force men like Jimmy Anderson back onto the streets of skid row.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:49.420
Soon the women and kids are going to take over the Rescue Union Mission

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.060
and all the men is going to have to move out there back out here on the street.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.960
The city's approach, known as Housing First, was adopted by municipalities nationwide

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:03.020
after Utah reportedly reduced chronic homelessness by 91%

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.820
by giving away permanent apartments with no strings attached.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.920
But state auditors later attributed those findings to a statistical error

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:17.240
and building housing for the homeless is considerably more costly and complex in Los Angeles

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.720
than in Salt Lake City.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.920
The city initially ballparked the permanent units at a median cost of $350,000 a piece.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:30.200
Three years later, the estimated costs rose to more than half a million per unit.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.520
Andy Bales says he saw it coming.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.310
You know I was a critic 10 years ago of this plan, even before it came about.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:43.560
It's a very expensive way of spending all the resources on a few and leaving the many out in the cold.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.460
Bales wanted the city to allocate a portion of the money to nonprofits and shelters like his

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.500
to provide temporary relief.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:56.120
And they laughed at me. They made fun of me and in the newspaper and here we are afterwards.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:02.760
There's this group that is so dogmatic about permanent supportive housing as the solution.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.120
They think everybody deserves a $600,000 unit with a granite countertop,

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.760
and anything short of that is not good enough.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.620
Bales says that given the current emergency, the city should reconsider its heavy focus

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.500
on finding a long-term solution.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:25.340
If we put four of these up in each district, 15 districts, 60 total, we could get, I want you to hear this,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.580
we could get 13,000 people off the street.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.920
Union Rescue Mission just opened what's called a Sprung Structure,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:36.340
a relatively inexpensive but sturdy and weather resistant tent with 120 beds.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:43.420
We cannot spend $600,000 per person per unit and ever get it done,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.460
but we could get there at $14,000 per bunk, in a Sprung Structure,

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:54.500
and we could get there at $50,000 mobile homes or $100,000 container homes

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:01.580
or $10,000 3-D printed concrete homes with a bathroom and kitchen and can be put up in 24 to 48 hours.

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:05.820
We've got to think innovatively or we're
going to have a bigger disaster on our hands.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.611
In terms of where to put these structures,

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.740
the city owns more than 7500 lots. Though neighborhood councils regularly fight

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.400
to keep shelters out.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.460
Property owners in Skid Row would like to see the police clear homeless encampments out of their neighborhood,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.220
which could also help to avert the public health crisis.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.040
But past court settlements prevent that,

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.860
and the September ruling on a case out of Idaho from the U.S. Ninth Circuit Appeals Court found that doing so

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:36.260
constitutes cruel and unusual punishment
when cities don't have adequate shelter

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.660
to accommodate everyone
living on the streets.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:43.320
Los Angeles city and county have signed
onto a lawsuit challenging that ruling.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.780
We just firmly believe that the police
are not an answer to homelessness.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:51.840
It should not be part of any kind of
homeless service system and definitely

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.680
should not be criminalizing
people for basic behaviors.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.200
Becky Dennison works for Venice
community housing corporation,

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.460
which opposes the criminalization
of sidewalk camping in Los Angeles.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.160
The idea that folks without housing are
out there by choice,

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:11.920
or not trying to get into housing and we just have to arrest people or nothing else will happen

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.180
is a complete false hood.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:19.120
At some point isn't there a conflict of rights between say a property owner or a business owner who

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.060
doesn't want tents right in front of their business and the ability of

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.120
someone to sleep on the sidewalk?

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:29.800
How do you reconcile that conflict?

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.571
Sidewalks should obviously
be available to everybody,

00:07:32.571 --> 00:07:35.180
so making sure they're passable
and all of those kinds of things.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:38.840
It's a way of sharing public space and
acknowledging that

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:42.240
some folks are living in our public spaces because
they have no place else to go.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.351
But proponents of the lawsuit say the
city needs guidance from the courts

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:51.260
on what constitutes adequate shelter before
investing in solutions that might free them

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.600
to enforce anti camping laws.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.660
If there was a push for
more temporary housing,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.260
let's say they built 30,000 beds,

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.360
would that justify
prohibiting sidewalk campaign?

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.351
I can't imagine it does. If
we ever get to that point,

00:08:06.351 --> 00:08:09.350
maybe that's a debate and a dialogue
we'll have to have as a society,

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.800
is there some level of control we want
to have over our streets and sidewalks?

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.320
I would say no. Maybe there's some
arguments to say yes, but we're not there.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:21.180
We have to focus on solutions and get
away from this idea that somehow

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:25.380
we'll be able to fix our neighborhood problems by being able to arrest people.

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:28.860
Given the lack of progress on permanent
or temporary housing,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:32.580
Bales is also hesitant to sign onto the
lawsuit for the time being.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:37.020
But I'm not signing on to remove people
from the streets until we have enough

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.280
places to go. When we get that,

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:44.540
then we can worry about asking somebody to leave the streets and go to this place.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:47.120
Under the increasing pressure in recent months,

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.400
the city has erected a few of its own
Sprung Structures to address the crisis.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.060
Bales says it's still not nearly enough.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.220
It's ridiculous. I mean,

00:08:55.220 --> 00:09:00.480
who would want to leave 44,000 people on the streets to die

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.900
while you stick with your very expensive plan to help a few?

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:11.020
We do have a FEMA-like, Red Cross-like crisis going on

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:19.740
with homelessness and it needs to be an immediate call to  action to get everybody who's willing under a roof.

